              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. In this case, the function `atoi()` is used, which can lead to integer overflow or underflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using unsafe C standard library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking or other forms of validation to prevent buffer overflows and other vulnerabilities. In the case of `atoi()`, consider using `strtol()` or `sscanf()`, which provide error checking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `atoi(argv[++i])` with a safer alternative. Here is an example using `strtol()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib> // for strtol\n#include <cerrno>  // for errno\n\nchar *end;\nlong val = strtol(argv[++i], &end, 10);\n\nif (end == argv[i] || *end != '\\0' || errno == ERANGE) {\n    // handle conversion error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the C standard library, which is typically included with the C++ compiler.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9f77c175-7e47-3867-9549-196a455675dc",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Identified\" is a vulnerability that arises when a program uses a C library function that is considered unsafe. These functions are often prohibited because they can lead to buffer overflows, integer overflows, or other types of vulnerabilities that can be exploited by an attacker.\n\nIn this case, the function `atoi()` is used, which converts a string to an integer. The problem with `atoi()` is that it does not perform any error checking. If the string cannot be converted to an integer, it returns zero, which can lead to unexpected behavior if not handled properly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should replace the `atoi()` function with a safer alternative that performs error checking. In C++, you can use the `std::stoi()` function, which throws an exception if the conversion fails.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can replace `atoi()` with `std::stoi()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\ntry {\n    int layer_mode = std::stoi(str_layer_mode);\n} catch (std::invalid_argument& e) {\n    // Handle exception\n} catch (std::out_of_range& e) {\n    // Handle exception\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`\n- `<cstdlib>`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "122852d0-7c5c-3649-bab4-8c83ab23225b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Identified\" is a vulnerability that arises when a program uses a C library function that is considered unsafe. These functions are often prohibited because they can lead to buffer overflows, integer overflows, or other types of vulnerabilities that can be exploited by an attacker.\n\nIn this case, the function `atoi()` is used, which converts a string to an integer. The problem with `atoi()` is that it does not perform any error checking. If the string cannot be converted to an integer, it returns zero, which can lead to unexpected behavior if not handled properly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should replace the `atoi()` function with a safer alternative that performs error checking. In C++, you can use the `std::stoi()` function, which throws an exception if the conversion fails.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is how you can replace `atoi()` with `std::stoi()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\ntry {\n    int layer_mode = std::stoi(str_layer_mode);\n} catch (std::invalid_argument& e) {\n    // Handle exception\n} catch (std::out_of_range& e) {\n    // Handle exception\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`\n- `<cstdlib>`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
